          "final_text_tail": ") accepts `cleanup_timeout: float | None = None`, stored as `self.cleanup_timeout`. Validation: must be a positive number; zero, negative, NaN, bool, or non-numeric values raise `ValueError(\"cleanup_timeout must be a positive number of seconds, got ...\")`; `None` = no timeout (default).\n- `Context.close()` (src/click/core.py:633) now routes through `_close_with_timeout()` when a timeout is set: cleanup runs in a daemon thread joined for at most `cleanup_timeout` seconds; on expiry a warning is logged via the `click` logger and the cleanup is abandoned (graceful degradation), so `close()` can never block longer than the timeout. If cleanup finishes in time, callback exceptions still propagate as before. `cleanup_timeout=None` keeps the original synchronous path (behavior unchanged).\n- Docstrings updated for the new parameter, `close()`, and a `versionchanged:: 8.2` note.\n\n`src/click/shell_completion.py`\n- `_resolve_context` wraps every `make_context` in `with` blocks so contexts are actually closed during completion (was already in the working tree), and now propagates `ctx.cleanup_timeout` to sub-contexts (both non-chain and chain paths), so the whole completion context hierarchy respects the timeout.\n\nVerified end-to-end: hanging `call_on_close` cleanups no longer block `close()`, `runner.invoke(cli, ..., cleanup_timeout=0.5)`, or `shell_complete(..., {\"cleanup_timeout\": 0.5}, ...)`; validation errors raise correctly; fast cleanups and exception propagation behave as before.",
